    {
      "id": "what-is-a-graph-section-5",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Why not just a page?",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": 80
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "title": "Why not just a page?",
        "body": "A page is the right form for something you read straight through, and Twilite keeps those too. A project is not read from top to bottom. What needs you today depends on which tasks are open, which questions are unanswered, and which goals they serve.\n\nIn a page, all of that lives in the prose, and you or your AI have to reread and re-sort it every time. A page expects one reading order. A graph has many paths: start from the overview, go into one branch, jump sideways to a related idea, and come back without losing the whole.\n\nIn a graph the relationships are already written down. Change one status and everything connected to it can see. Your AI can add a task without rewriting your document. And a graph still has a page: when you are close enough to read it, you see its first face.",
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "what-is-a-graph"
        },
        "classRef": "tlz://twilite.zone/library/templates/page-template/classes/page-section.node-class.node"
      },
